From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: describe the USB hub and the WiFi module

The USB-A port J8 and the WiFi/BT module are behind a WCH CH334F hub (U9)
on USB1. The hub is fed from DCDC1 (VCC_3V3_USB20HUB, R44 0R) and has no
reset line: XRSTJ is wired to unfitted parts only. PM5 does not power the
hub, it only enables U10, the VBUS switch of J8.

The module U3 (AIC8800D80) sits on port 1 of the hub. Its supply WIFI_3V3
is the output of load switch Q8, enabled by USB_WIFI_PWR (PM0); its CHIP_EN
pin is WL-REG-ON (PM1), active high.

Until now the kernel did not control the power of the module: PM0 and PM1
were two always-on regulators, and the hub had no node. Describe both
devices, so that onboard_usb_dev owns the module's supply and its reset line
and releases reset 14 ms after enabling the supply (a fixed delay of the
onboard_usb_dev entry, not measured): a hub@1 node on ehci1 with its two supplies from DCDC1, and the module as wifi@1 below it,
with WIFI_3V3 as a plain regulator-fixed on PM0 and CHIP_EN as its
reset-gpios on PM1. This replaces `arm64-dts-a733-power-the-onboard-wifi-module`;
one rail has one owner.

The hub itself is not switched by this: DCDC1 stays on and the hub has no
reset line, so onboard_usb_dev cannot power it off or reset it. The patch
does not address the error that the hub's first enumeration used to show,

  usb 3-1: device descriptor read/64, error -71

and makes no claim about it.

DCDC1 gets a node, always on and without a voltage window like the other
rails of the PMIC, because onboard_usb_dev disables the hub's supplies when
the hub goes away.

The comment on the PM5 regulator said it powers the hub and the module. It
only switches VBUS of J8.

Needs the CH334 and AIC8800D80 entries of onboard_usb_dev and the matching
bindings, and USB_ONBOARD_DEV in the config. The Bluetooth function of the
module needs `bluetooth-btusb-request-the-reset-gpio-only-where-a-handler-uses-it`,
because btusb would otherwise request the reset line that onboard_usb_dev
holds.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -528,9 +528,10 @@ reg_vcc3v3: regulator-vcc3v3 {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * VCC5V0_USB20 -> the CH334F hub (USB-A J8 + on-board WiFi/BT), switched by
-	 * SGM2576 U10 whose enable is USB_HOST_EN = PM5 (schematic p7:172 / p13:92).
-	 * Held on: the hub must be powered for J8 and the WiFi/BT module to work.
+	 * VCC5V0_USB20 -> VBUS of the USB-A port J8, switched by SGM2576 U10
+	 * whose enable is USB_HOST_EN = PM5 (schematic p7:172 / p13:92). The
+	 * CH334F hub U9 and the WiFi/BT module do not hang on it, see the hub
+	 * node on ehci1. Held on: nothing consumes it, and J8 is a host port.
 	 * PM5 lives on the AON S_GPIO bank (r_pio), bank index 1 (PL=0, PM=1).
 	 */
 	reg_usb_host_vbus: regulator-usb-host-vbus {
@@ -543,6 +544,20 @@ reg_usb_host_vbus: regulator-usb-host-vbus {
 		regulator-always-on;
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * WIFI_3V3 of the WiFi/BT module: load switch Q8 (WPM2015), enabled by
+	 * USB_WIFI_PWR = PM0. Not always on: onboard_usb_dev switches it before
+	 * the hub port of the module is enumerated.
+	 */
+	reg_wifi_3v3: regulator-wifi-3v3 {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "wifi-3v3";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		gpio = <&r_pio 1 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+	};
+
 	/*
 	 * VBUS for the Type-C J4 SuperSpeed port, switched by SGM2576 U2 whose
 	 * enable is USB0-DRVVBUS = PL2 (the "USB0" net name is a schematic label;
@@ -1375,7 +1390,37 @@ ehci1: usb@4200000 {
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_OHCI1>, <&ccu RST_BUS_EHCI1>;
 			phys = <&usbphy 1>;
 			phy-names = "usb";
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <0>;
 			status = "okay";
+
+			/*
+			 * CH334F hub U9: VD33 and VDD5 from DCDC1 (R44 0R, L13
+			 * not fitted), always powered. XRSTJ has no SoC pin:
+			 * only unfitted parts, the hub resets itself at power
+			 * on.
+			 */
+			hub@1 {
+				compatible = "usb1a86,8091";
+				reg = <1>;
+				#address-cells = <1>;
+				#size-cells = <0>;
+				vdd33-supply = <&reg_dcdc1>;
+				v5-supply = <&reg_dcdc1>;
+
+				/*
+				 * AIC8800D80 WiFi/BT module U3 (FCU760K) on port 1,
+				 * soldered. CHIP_EN is WL-REG-ON = PM1, active
+				 * high: onboard_usb_dev holds it low until the
+				 * supply is stable, then releases it.
+				 */
+				wifi@1 {
+					compatible = "usba69c,8d80";
+					reg = <1>;
+					vdd-supply = <&reg_wifi_3v3>;
+					reset-gpios = <&r_pio 1 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+				};
+			};
 		};
 
 		ohci1: usb@4200400 {
@@ -2499,6 +2544,18 @@ pmic@36 {
 				#interrupt-cells = <1>;
 
 				regulators {
+					/*
+					 * VCC-3V3-SYS: the 3.3 V system rail, among others the
+					 * supply of the USB hub U9 (VCC_3V3_USB20HUB). Always on:
+					 * the hub driver switches its supplies with the hub, and
+					 * this one must not go. Like the other rails here it
+					 * carries no voltage window.
+					 */
+					reg_dcdc1: dcdc1 {
+						regulator-name = "vcc-3v3-sys";
+						regulator-always-on;
+					};
+
 					/*
 					 * The two CPU DVFS rails. They get a DT node --
 					 * and therefore a window, and therefore the right
